const WALK = {
  ask: 'why does my character move faster diagonally, and how do I fix it?',
  file: 'Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs',
  label: 'In the Code',
  intro:
    'One small change: the two axes get their own fields instead of being multiplied inside the movement line. Everything else in `Update()` is your 1.4 code, unchanged.',
  level: 1,
  steps: [
    {
      title: 'Give the two axes their own fields',
      lead: 'In the class body, under `yDirection`.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// PlayerController.cs — the class body, below the two direction fields.', what: '' },
        { code: '    float xVector;', what: 'The horizontal part of the movement in units per second. A field rather than a local, because 1.6 reads it to choose an animation and 2.2 hands it to the physics body.' },
        { code: '    float yVector;', what: 'The same for vertical. Two numbers that will be handed around as a pair.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '// PlayerController.cs — inside Update(), after the two reads.', what: '' },
        { code: '        xVector = xDirection * speed;', what: 'Turns "pressing right" into "four units per second to the right". Exactly the multiplication 1.4 did inside the move line, moved up and given a name.' },
        { code: '        yVector = yDirection * speed;', what: 'The same for up/down.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '        transform.Translate(new Vector2(xVector, yVector) * Time.deltaTime);', what: 'The line from 1.4, one term shorter: the speed is already in `xVector` and `yVector`, so only the frame time is left to multiply.' , why: '**Say what this line does without reading it aloud:** move by (units per second right, units per second up) scaled to the frame I just had. Three separate questions — which way, how fast, how long — and now each one has its own name.' },
        { code: '    }', what: '' },
      ],
    },
  ],
  finalFile: `using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    float speed = 4;        // units per second — you tune this today

    float xDirection;       // -1, 0 or 1 from the keyboard
    float yDirection;
    float xVector;          // the same thing in units per second
    float yVector;

    void Update()
    {
        xDirection = Input.GetAxisRaw("Horizontal");
        yDirection = Input.GetAxisRaw("Vertical");

        xVector = xDirection * speed;
        yVector = yDirection * speed;

        transform.Translate(new Vector2(xVector, yVector) * Time.deltaTime);
    }
}`,
  finalNote:
    'Compare it against your own file. The difference from 1.4 should be exactly three lines: two fields added, and the multiplication lifted out of the movement line. If you also added a `.normalized` line, you have taken the fix — that is a defensible choice, and it is not the one this project made today. Be able to say which you did and why.',
};

const body = `<h1>1.5 — Movement II: Speed, deltaTime &amp; Tuning</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">Your character moves, and one thing about that is wrong: hold two direction keys at once and it travels 41% faster. Today you measure that, name the one line that fixes it, and then make a decision about it — which is not the same thing as failing to fix it.</p>

${renderAskBanner('in unity how to use time.deltatime, how to normalize a movement vector, how to stop diagonal movement being faster', 1)}

${renderDecompose('1.5', DECOMPOSE, 1)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span><span class="activity-time">~20 min</span></div>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Scripts</code>, and open <code>PlayerController.cs</code> — the file you built in 1.4.</li>
        <li><strong>Game view →</strong> press <strong>Play</strong> and hold <strong>D</strong> for two seconds, then <strong>W and D together</strong> for two seconds. Put a landmark on screen and race it. The diagonal covers visibly more ground — you can see the bug before you write anything.</li>
        <li><strong>Project window → PlayerController.cs</strong>, and read the movement line. <code>xDirection</code> and <code>yDirection</code> are each multiplied by <code>speed</code>. Nothing on that line says how far <em>two</em> of them together are.</li>
        <li><strong>Project window → PlayerController.cs</strong>, change <code>speed</code> to <code>2</code>, save, and press <strong>Play</strong> again. Then <code>6</code>, or <code>7</code>. The number lives in the script in this project — tuning is an edit and a new play, not an Inspector box.</li>
        <li><strong>GitHub Desktop →</strong> commit and push: the two new fields, and the speed you decided on.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Play mode is a dream, edit mode is reality</strong> (1.2). A value changed while the Play button is blue resets when you stop — but a value changed in the <em>script</em> is a saved file, and it survives everything.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">In the Code</span><span class="activity-time">~15 min</span></div>
    <p>Unity offers four ways to move an object, each because it handles something the others handle badly. You use the first today.</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Technique</th><th>How it works</th><th>Needs a Rigidbody2D?</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
        <tr><td><code>transform.Translate(vector)</code></td><td><strong>Offsets coordinates directly</strong>, skipping the physics engine.</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>Simple top-down games, moving UI, linear projectiles</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Rigidbody2D.MovePosition(position)</code></td><td><strong>Drives the body to a target position</strong>, so whatever is in the way gets a say: a <strong>Dynamic</strong> body is stopped by a solid collider, a Kinematic one arrives anyway.</td><td>Yes — <strong>Dynamic</strong> if you want a wall to stop it</td><td>Moving platforms and scripted set pieces that must arrive at an exact position</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity</code></td><td><strong>Assigns a velocity</strong> and lets the physics engine move the body. The number is per <em>second</em>, not per frame.</td><td>Yes — <strong>Dynamic</strong> if you want a wall to stop it</td><td>Snappy platformers, arcade physics, momentum across surfaces</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Rigidbody2D.AddForce(vector)</code></td><td><strong>Applies a physical push</strong>, factoring in mass, drag and inertia.</td><td>Yes — <strong>Dynamic</strong>; a Kinematic body ignores forces</td><td>Jumping, thrusters, ice physics, knockback</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="callout warn">
        <strong>Do not switch methods today.</strong> Three of these need a Rigidbody2D your Player does not have, and you will not add one yet: 2.1 adds it for a different purpose (coins) and 2.2 is where it stops you walking through walls.
    </div>
    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Choosing the Right Tool for the Job</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>You proved in <a href="1.4.html">1.4</a> that the translate call walks through walls, while <code>Rigidbody2D.MovePosition(position)</code> is stopped by one — on a <strong>Dynamic</strong> body. What does that call consult that the Transform never does?</li>
            <li>Three of these four need a component your Player does not have. What would you add, and where — on the Player, or on the child Sprite?</li>
            <li>Why would a spaceship drifting through space prefer <code>Rigidbody2D.AddForce(vector)</code> to the translate call?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">Known Debt: The 41% You Are Not Fixing</span><span class="activity-time">~20 min</span></div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — see the number.</strong> Add one line to <code>Update()</code>, play, and
        hold one key, then two:
    </p>

<pre><code class="lang-cs">        print(new Vector2(xDirection, yDirection).magnitude);</code></pre>

    <p>
        One key reads <code>1</code>. Two keys read <code>1.414</code>. That number is
        <code>√2</code> — the diagonal of a one-by-one square — and it is exactly why your character
        covers 41% more ground when you press two keys. Nothing is broken; a vector's
        <strong>length</strong> is not the same as its <strong>direction</strong>, and you have been
        multiplying speed by a length that changes.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — see the one-line fix.</strong> This is the line that would remove all 41% of
        it, in one place, for every direction at once:
    </p>

<pre><code class="lang-cs">        Vector2 direction = new Vector2(xDirection, yDirection).normalized;</code></pre>

    <p>
        <code>.normalized</code> keeps the arrow pointing the same way and rescales its length to
        exactly <code>1</code>. Two keys become <code>(0.707, 0.707)</code> instead of
        <code>(1, 1)</code>, which is still diagonal and still the right shape — 0.707 is
        <code>1 ÷ √2</code>. Multiply that by <code>speed</code> and every direction costs the same.
    </p>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>And now the decision: we are not taking it today.</strong> This game is top-down, the
        diagonal is not ruining anything yet, and the fix changes how the character feels — which is
        the kind of change you want to make on purpose, with a playtest before and after, not at the
        end of a lesson. So the line is written down here, the debt has a name, and it goes on the
        board. <strong>A known bug you chose is not the same as a bug you never found</strong>, and the
        difference is this paragraph.
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Take the <code>print</code> back out.</strong> It was an instrument, and it prints
        once per frame — sixty lines a second — forever. Instruments come out before you commit, or
        they become the next thing somebody has to debug.
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 3 — choose the speed.</strong> With the debt named, spend the rest of the hour on
        the number that actually decides how the game feels. <code>speed</code> starts at
        <code>4</code>. Try <code>2</code>, <code>4</code>, <code>7</code>: too low is sluggish, too
        high is twitchy, and there is no correct answer — only the one you can defend against a
        landmark in your level. Write it down, and say why in one sentence.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 4 — catch up.</strong> Anyone who has not finished 1.4 or 1.3 uses this time.
        Anyone who has: place a few landmarks in the Overworld and walk the perimeter, checking the
        speed against the size of the room the player actually moves in.
    </p>
</div>

